<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peacock Throne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peacock Throne was a jewelled throne made for the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> in the seventeenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> carried it off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1739, along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a> diamond, after his invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was destroyed after Nader's death, and it symbolises the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>'s plunder and the impoverishment of the Mughal state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peacock-throne.jpg" alt="Peacock Throne" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Mughal_Emperor_and_His_Court,_c._1774_-_V%26A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> ruled from the newly built capital of Shahjahanabad, and his court was meant to be a mirror of paradise on earth. A ruler at its centre would be worthy of a Throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, covered in gold and jewels and approached by steps, so that he seemed to float above the ground. Said Gilani and workers of the imperial goldsmiths' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> built it in seven years, at twice the cost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a>. It stood in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">Diwan</a>-i-Khas in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-fort">Red Fort</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><p>It was first called the Jeweled Throne, Takht-Murassa, and later historians named it from the peacock figures on its canopy. Its inauguration in March 1635 was set by astrologers for a day that coincided with Eid al-Fitr and Nowruz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>No painting matching the early descriptions is known. The Mughal historians Abdul Hamid Lahori and Inayat Khan describe a throne with a canopy on twelve pillars, set with rubies, garnets, diamonds, pearls and emeralds, with peacocks on the pillars, reached by three jewelled steps. Lahori names the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a> and the Timur Ruby among its stones.</p><p>François Bernier saw the emperor seated on it and put its value at four crores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. Jean-Baptiste Tavernier inspected it in 1665 and counted 108 large balas rubies and 116 large emeralds, but named none of the great historic stones, which may have been with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, then confined at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>. Tavernier is considered among the least reliable sources from a conventional historical perspective.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> took the original in 1739 after his invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with the Koh-i Noor and Darya-i Noor diamonds, and his plunder was so great that he stopped taxation for three years. Another throne was made for the Mughal emperor, shown in 19th-century Indian paintings, which disappeared during or soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>. Part of the original may have been converted into the Sun Throne of the Persian crown jewels. Later Shahs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> were sometimes said to hold the Peacock Throne, symbolically. In the 1860s King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a> had a romanticised version built in the Moorish Kiosk at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linderhof-palace">Linderhof Palace</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peacock Throne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Peacock Throne</b> and the Koh-i-Noor and Darya-i-Noor diamonds were among the prizes carried back to Persia, together with large numbers of artisans and craftsmen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>External invasions, by Nader Shah of Persia, who sacked Delhi in 1739 and carried off the <b>Peacock Throne</b>, and later by Ahmad Shah Durrani, further destabilised the empire and depleted its wealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
